window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][34] = "<p>Section 4 sets out the answer architecture for platform questions, with types for growing a marketplace, an API product, platform policy, a key participant turning competitor, and platform metrics.</p>\n<span id=\"c19-c19-9-worked-examples\"></span>\n<h4>C19.9 Worked examples</h4>\n<p>Section 5 gives five worked examples: an API's developer funnel, an openness decision, a take-rate analysis, a cold start by density and platform health metrics. Section 11 adds seven questions with model answers, including questions set on Apple, Microsoft and Stripe products.</p>\n<span id=\"c19-3-frameworks\"></span>\n<h3>3. Frameworks</h3>\n<p>Eight frameworks, set out below. The sources-of-advantage test (FW-C18-05), the strategy spine (FW-C18-02), the unit-economics sketch (FW-C18-06), the metric card (FW-C05-01), the rollout ladder (FW-C17-02) and the ethical nudge test (FW-C10-06) are reused, not repeated.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c19-01\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C19-01 · The platform map · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a platform and its participants.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a map of sides: what each gives and gets, alternatives, fears, and the interface between them (rules, API, payments); the value loop; the first constraint.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> you can name the sides and their motives.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a map simplifies; add sub-sides (large and small developers).</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> one-sided design; a label with no mechanism; forgetting regulators and users.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the map in Worked examples 1 and 3 and CS-C19-02.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Side</th>\n<th>Gives</th>\n<th>Gets</th>\n<th>Alternatives</th>\n<th>Fears</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c19-02\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C19-02 · The platform type chooser · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a product that others build on or transact through.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a classification (two-sided market, developer platform, or both) with the problems each brings and the main metric.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the participants are identifiable.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> hybrids carry both sets of problems.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> calling a product a platform without outside builders or counterparties.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> IQ-C19-01.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Core job</th>\n<th>Hard problem</th>\n<th>Primary metric</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Two-sided market</td>\n<td>Match and build trust</td>\n<td>Cold start; liquidity; disintermediation</td>\n<td>Successful transactions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Developer platform</td>\n<td>Be a reliable, easy foundation</td>\n<td>Developer experience; stability; governance</td>\n<td>Active developers shipping to production</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hybrid (e.g., app store)</td>\n<td>Both</td>\n<td>Review policy; fees; conflict with participants</td>\n<td>Value to both sides together</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c19-03\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C19-03 · The cold-start playbook · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a platform with no network.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a plan: single-player value, a narrow starting market, which side to seed and how, a borrowed network if any, and a liquidity target with the density needed.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> a match probability per supplier can be estimated.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a narrow start limits the early market and creates density.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> spreading thin; counting sign-ups; no plan to seed the hard side.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 4 and CS-C19-01.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Formula: <strong>P(match) = 1 − (1 − p)ᴺ</strong>, where p is the chance one supplier fulfills a request and N is the suppliers in the market.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c19-04\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C19-04 · The developer-experience funnel · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a developer product.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a funnel (discover, sign up, credentials, first successful call, production), time to first call, and the friction at each step with fixes.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the steps can be instrumented.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> optimizing the first mile helps new developers and may neglect experienced ones.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> measuring sign-ups; ignoring error messages; breaking changes without notice.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 1.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"fw-c19-05\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C19-05 · The governance dial · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a platform rule or fee decision.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> the options along speed, safety and developer trust (and openness and price), the effect on each side, a recommendation with measures and a review date.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> effects on each side can be reasoned about or tested.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> every setting helps one side and costs another.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> deciding for one side; no appeals path; fee changes without transition.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked examples 2 and 3 and IQ-C19-05.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Dial</th>\n<th>Toward one end</th>\n<th>Toward the other</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Review</td>\n<td>Strict: safety, slower</td>\n<td>Light: speed, risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Openness</td>\n<td>Closed: consistency</td>\n<td>Open: innovation, risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Take rate</td>\n<td>High: funding</td>\n<td>Low: participation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c19-06\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C19-06 · The ecosystem risk scan · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a platform and its participants.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a table of risks (dependency, concentration, disintermediation, conflict with participants, regulatory, security, multi-homing) with likelihood, impact, an indicator and a mitigation.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> indicators can be measured.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> mitigations cost revenue or control.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> a list without indicators; ignoring concentration.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the scan in CS-C19-02 and IQ-C19-06.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Risk</th>\n<th>Indicator</th>\n<th>Mitigation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c19-07\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C19-07 · The platform health metrics set · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a platform and its core value.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a primary metric tied to successful transactions or shipped integrations, metrics for supply, demand, interaction, quality, economics and ecosystem health, and guardrails.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> each side's activity is observable.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> many metrics invite confusion; keep one primary and a few guardrails.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> a single volume metric; no quality or concentration measure.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 5.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"fw-c19-08\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C19-08 · The review-policy triangle · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a platform that reviews submissions.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> targets for speed (time to decision), safety (harm prevented) and developer trust (consistency, clarity, appeals), a tiered process, and the measures that show the balance.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> submissions can be sorted by risk.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> improving two usually costs the third.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> one blanket process; opaque rejections; no appeals; no measure of false rejections.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> IQ-C19-05.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Steps: <strong>Sort submissions by risk → Automate routine checks → Human review for high risk → Explain every rejection → Offer appeals → Measure speed, harm and false rejections.</strong></p>\n<span id=\"c19-4-answer-architecture\"></span>\n<h3>4. Answer architecture</h3>\n<span id=\"c19-the-platform-answer-for-a-25-minute-round\"></span>\n<h4>The platform answer, for a 25-minute round</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Minutes</th>\n<th>What to produce</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Clarify the platform and the goal</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>What it is, the sides, the stage (launch, growth, maturity), the objective</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Map the sides and the loop</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>The platform map: what each side gives and gets; the flywheel; the first constraint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stage-specific plan</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Cold start (density, seed, narrow) or growth or mature governance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Governance and economics</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Rules and review; take rate or pricing with a response model; openness; developer experience</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Risks and metrics</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>The ecosystem risk scan; platform health metrics with guardrails</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Decision and sequence</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>The recommendation, first steps, what would change it</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Pacing rules.</strong> If the question names the stage (cold start, a policy), spend the time there. If you run behind, keep the map, the main tradeoff and one metric. Treat questions as steering.</p>\n<p><strong>How to say it.</strong> Name the sides first: &quot;There are three groups: developers, users and the platform. Each gives and gets...&quot; State the tradeoff with a number where you can.</p>\n<p><strong>How to close.</strong> Two sentences: &quot;I'd start by [first step], governed by [rule], measured by [primary metric] with guardrails on [guardrails]. The main ecosystem risk is [risk], and I'd change course if [signal].&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c19-type-grow-a-marketplace-from-nothing\"></span>\n<h4>Type: grow a marketplace from nothing</h4>\n<p>Use the cold-start playbook with the density arithmetic.</p>\n<span id=\"c19-type-an-api-or-developer-product\"></span>\n<h4>Type: an API or developer product</h4>\n<p>Use the developer-experience funnel and stability; metrics: time to first call, production share.</p>\n<span id=\"c19-type-platform-policy-review-fees-openness\"></span>\n<h4>Type: platform policy (review, fees, openness)</h4>\n<p>Use the governance dial and the review-policy triangle; model the effect on each side.</p>\n<span id=\"c19-type-a-key-participant-becomes-a-competitor\"></span>\n<h4>Type: a key participant becomes a competitor</h4>\n<p>Use the risk scan: dependency, conflict of interest, trust; options and safeguards.</p>\n<span id=\"c19-type-platform-metrics\"></span>\n<h4>Type: platform metrics</h4>\n<p>Primary metric plus sides plus guardrails; include concentration.</p>\n<span id=\"c19-type-the-short-version-10-minutes\"></span>\n<h4>Type: the short version (10 minutes)</h4>\n<p>Sides, the loop, the top tradeoff, one number. Protect the tradeoff.</p>\n<span id=\"c19-common-interviewer-interventions-and-what-to-do\"></span>\n<h4>Common interviewer interventions, and what to do</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Interviewer says</th>\n<th>What it means</th>\n<th>What to do</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Who are the sides?&quot;</td>\n<td>You named one</td>\n<td>Map each side's gives, gets and fears</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Why will it take off?&quot;</td>\n<td>The network-effect label</td>\n<td>Name the mechanism, its locality and saturation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;How many sellers do you need?&quot;</td>\n<td>Density</td>\n<td>Use the match probability and a threshold</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Developers say it's hard to start.&quot;</td>\n<td>Developer experience</td>\n<td>Funnel and time to first call</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;What if they leave?&quot;</td>\n<td>Dependency and multi-homing</td>\n<td>Concentration, switching costs, incentives</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;What does the platform charge?&quot;</td>\n<td>Take rate</td>\n<td>Model transaction value response; tiering</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;What about regulators?&quot;</td>\n<td>Regulatory risk</td>\n<td>Name exposure and design with it; mark facts to verify</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c19-5-worked-examples\"></span>\n<h3>5. Worked examples</h3>\n<p>All five examples are <code>Illustrative</code>: the products are fictional or use a product type as the setting only, and every figure is an invented assumption. The arithmetic was computed. Each is written as notes you could say aloud.</p>\n<span id=\"c19-worked-example-1-a-payments-api-improving-the-developer-s-fi\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 1 — A payments API: improving the developer's first mile (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><em>Setting: a payments API of the kind Stripe offers. No fact about any real company is claimed.</em></p>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> Developers say integration is painful. The PM starts by measuring the funnel (FW-C19-04). Of 10,000 developers who sign up in a month (invented): 7,600 get credentials (76%), 5,320 make a first successful call (70% of those), and 3,000 reach production (56% of those). Overall, 30% of sign-ups ship. The median time from sign-up to first successful call is 4 hours.</p>\n<p><strong>Find the friction at each step.</strong> Session recordings and support tickets (read by the PM and an engineer): at credentials, developers confuse test and live keys; at the first call, the quick-start example uses an outdated parameter, so the first request returns an error that says only &quot;invalid request&quot;; at production, developers do not know what to test before going live and hit surprise review steps.</p>\n<p><strong>Fixes, in order of effect.</strong> (1) Make the first example work as written and test it on every release. (2) Error messages that say what is wrong and how to fix it, with a link (&quot;The 'amount' field must be an integer in the smallest currency unit; see the guide&quot;). (3) Label test and live keys clearly and default to test. (4) A go-live checklist and a sandbox that mimics production. (5) SDKs in the languages most developers use.</p>\n<p><strong>What improvement looks like.</strong> If the three steps rise to 82%, 85% and 72%, the end-to-end share is 10,000 × 0.82 × 0.85 × 0.72 = 5,018, or 50%, up from 30%. The median time to first call falls to under an hour as the target (invented). The metric to own is time to first successful call, with the share reaching production as the outcome.</p>\n<p><strong>Stability as a feature.</strong> Developers build on the interface, so changes must not break them: version the API, announce deprecations months ahead, and support old versions for a published period. Breaking changes without notice would cost more trust than any documentation gain.</p>\n<p><strong>Guardrails.</strong> Support tickets per developer should fall; API error rates for correct requests should not rise; fraud and abuse checks must not slow legitimate developers.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Treating the API as a product with a funnel found specific, fixable friction, and the metric (time to first call) pointed to the first mile.</p>\n<span id=\"c19-worked-example-2-an-openness-decision-for-a-mobile-operating\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 2 — An openness decision for a mobile operating system (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><em>Setting: a mobile operating system platform of the kind Google develops. No fact about any real company is claimed.</em></p>\n<p><strong>The question.</strong> The platform owner licenses its operating system to many device makers. How much freedom should device makers have to modify it?</p>\n<p><strong>Sides (FW-C19-01).</strong> <em>Device makers</em> want freedom to differentiate and low cost. <em>Developers</em> want apps to run the same on every device. <em>Users</em> want a safe, consistent, secure device. <em>The platform owner</em> wants wide adoption, a healthy app ecosystem and a way to fund the work.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong> (A) Fully open: any modification, no requirements. (B) A compatibility program: device makers may customize, but must pass tests of compatibility and security to use the platform's brand, services and app store. (C) Closed: only the platform owner makes devices.</p>\n<p><strong>Scoring (FW-C19-05).</strong> Criteria and weights (invented): partner adoption 25%, developer consistency 25%, security 20%, innovation 15%, control and revenue 15%. Scores 1 to 5:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Adoption</th>\n<th>Consistency</th>\n<th>Security</th>\n<th>Innovation</th>\n<th>Control</th>\n<th>Weighted</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Open</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>3.05</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Compatibility program</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>3.85</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Closed</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>3.80</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Flip test.</strong> Weight adoption more (35%, 20%, 15%, 15%, 15%): open 3.35, compatibility 3.85, closed 3.50: compatibility stays first. Weight consistency and security more (15%, 30%, 30%, 10%, 15%): open 2.60, compatibility 3.85, closed 4.25: closed wins. So the compatibility program is the robust choice unless consistency and security dominate, and that is exactly the question to settle with the leadership: how much do we value reach against uniform quality?</p>\n<p><strong>Details of the middle path.</strong> What must devices pass (compatibility tests, security updates for a stated period)? What do device makers get in return (the brand, services, store access)? What happens to a device maker that fails (a path to fix before sanctions)? How are updates delivered to the long tail of devices? These are the governance decisions that make the middle path real.</p>\n<p><strong>Risks.</strong> Fragmentation if tests are weak; resentment if the program is arbitrary; regulatory scrutiny if the program is seen as tying services to terms <code>[V: Apple Form 10-Q, 2026, on US and EU proceedings over app-store terms]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Recommendation.</strong> The compatibility program, with transparent tests, a published timeline for updates, and a review point at which the balance is revisited with data on fragmentation, security incidents and partner satisfaction. What would change my mind: a security crisis (move toward closed), or a collapse in partner adoption (move toward open).</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> A weighted score with a flip test showed which choice was robust and what the real debate was about.</p>\n<span id=\"c19-worked-example-3-setting-a-take-rate-for-an-enterprise-marke\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 3 — Setting a take rate for an enterprise marketplace (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><em>Setting: a marketplace in an enterprise software suite of the kind Microsoft offers. No fact about any real company is claimed.</em></p>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> A marketplace lets independent software vendors sell add-ons to the suite's customers. The PM must propose a take rate. Operating the marketplace (payments, security review, support, hosting) costs about 8% of the value of transactions (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>How participation responds (invented).</strong> At a 20% take rate, the value of transactions through the marketplace is 100 (index). At 15%, more vendors list and prices are lower: 108. At 10%, 118. At 5%, 125.</p>\n<p><strong>The economics (FW-C19-05).</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Take rate</th>\n<th>Transaction value</th>\n<th>Revenue</th>\n<th>Operating cost (8% of value)</th>\n<th>Contribution</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>20%</td>\n<td>100</td>\n<td>20.0</td>\n<td>8.0</td>\n<td>12.0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>15%</td>\n<td>108</td>\n<td>16.2</td>\n<td>8.6</td>\n<td>7.6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10%</td>\n<td>118</td>\n<td>11.8</td>\n<td>9.4</td>\n<td>2.4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5%</td>\n<td>125</td>\n<td>6.3</td>\n<td>10.0</td>\n<td>−3.8</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Reading it.</strong> Revenue and contribution fall as the rate falls, since the increase in transaction value does not make up the lost share. Below about 8% the marketplace loses money on each transaction. A lower rate is justified only if it buys something the numbers above do not capture: faster ecosystem growth that makes the core suite more valuable (the platform earns from the suite, not only from the marketplace), a defense against competing marketplaces, or regulatory pressure.</p>\n<p><strong>The strategic view.</strong> If the marketplace's role is to make the suite stickier and more valuable, its contribution should be judged together with the suite's revenue and retention, not alone. Suppose the add-ons raise suite retention enough to be worth 10 (index) at a 10% rate; then the 10% marketplace is worth 2.4 + 10 = 12.4, comparable with the 12.0 at 20%. That is an assumption to test, not a fact.</p>\n<p><strong>Tiering.</strong> A common design: a standard rate (say 20%), a reduced rate for small vendors or for vendors that use the platform's billing and co-selling (say 10%), and a transition period for any change. This protects small vendors, rewards deeper integration and avoids a cliff.</p>\n<p><strong>Recommendation.</strong> Start at 20% with a 10% tier for small vendors and co-sold sales, measure vendor growth and suite retention for two quarters, and review. Communicate rules and changes with notice. What would change my mind: evidence that vendors are leaving for a rival marketplace, or that add-ons lift suite retention by more than assumed.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> A small model made the floor (cost) and the trade (rate against participation) visible, and tied the decision to the platform's wider value.</p>\n<span id=\"c19-worked-example-4-the-cold-start-density-before-breadth-illus\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 4 — The cold start: density before breadth (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> A marketplace connects students with tutors who can come at short notice. The team has recruited 600 tutors and is deciding where to launch. A request is matched if at least one nearby tutor is available and accepts; suppose each tutor does so with probability 5% for a given request (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>The math (FW-C19-03).</strong> The chance of a match with N tutors is 1 − 0.95ᴺ: 20 tutors give 64%; 40 give 87%; 45 give 90%; 60 give 95%. To reach a 90% match rate, a market needs at least 45 tutors (ln 0.1 ÷ ln 0.95 ≈ 44.9).</p>\n<p><strong>Two plans.</strong> <em>Spread:</em> launch in 30 cities with 20 tutors each. Each city has a 64% match rate. More than a third of requests fail, and students who fail once rarely return. <em>Concentrate:</em> launch in 13 cities with 45 tutors each (600 ÷ 45 = 13.3). Each city has a 90% match rate. The second plan gives a product that works where it launches, with word of mouth that compounds.</p>\n<p><strong>Seeding and single-player value.</strong> To recruit supply before demand exists, offer tutors something useful on their own (a scheduling and payment tool, a guaranteed minimum for the first months). Narrow further by subject or by school to raise the chance of a match for the students who come first.</p>\n<p><strong>What to measure.</strong> Not sign-ups, but liquidity: match rate, time to match, and the share of students who book a second session. Set a gate: expand to a new city only when the first reaches a 90% match rate and a repeat rate above a threshold.</p>\n<p><strong>Risks.</strong> Tutors and students may transact off the platform after the first match (disintermediation); mitigate with value they cannot get elsewhere (payment protection, scheduling, reviews). Quality and safety (verification of tutors) must be in place before launch, since one bad incident is costly.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The arithmetic of matching turned &quot;launch everywhere&quot; into &quot;launch where density is achievable,&quot; and gave a gate for expansion.</p>\n<span id=\"c19-worked-example-5-reading-platform-health-metrics-illustrativ\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 5 — Reading platform health metrics (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> A marketplace reports a monthly review (invented). Orders rose from 120,000 to 127,000 (+5.8%), at an average value of $35, so transaction value rose from $4.20 million to $4.45 million. The leadership is pleased.</p>\n<p><strong>The full table (FW-C19-07).</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Metric</th>\n<th>Last month</th>\n<th>This month</th>\n<th>Reading</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Orders</td>\n<td>120,000</td>\n<td>127,000</td>\n<td>Up 5.8%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Active sellers</td>\n<td>5,000</td>\n<td>5,100</td>\n<td>Up 2%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Active buyers</td>\n<td>80,000</td>\n<td>83,200</td>\n<td>Up 4%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Match rate (requests that lead to an order)</td>\n<td>62%</td>\n<td>58%</td>\n<td>Down 4 points</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Time to first response</td>\n<td>11 min</td>\n<td>16 min</td>\n<td>Slower</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Disputes per 1,000 orders</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>Worse</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Share of orders from the top 10 sellers</td>\n<td>36%</td>\n<td>40%</td>\n<td>More concentrated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Take rate</td>\n<td>14%</td>\n<td>14%</td>\n<td>Flat</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Reading it.</strong> Growth is real in volume. But demand grew faster than supply (buyers +4%, sellers +2%), the match rate fell by 4 points, response time lengthened by 5 minutes, disputes rose by more than a third, and orders concentrated further in a few sellers. The platform is growing by pushing more buyers into a supply base that is not keeping up: a pattern that tends to precede lower repeat rates and seller churn. The transaction-value headline hides it.</p>\n<p><strong>What I'd do.</strong> Make match rate (successful transactions per request) the primary metric, with disputes, time to response and concentration as guardrails. Investigate the dispute rise (a new seller group? a category?). Boost supply where the match rate is lowest. Watch repeat purchase and seller churn next month for confirmation.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> A set of metrics across sides, with a primary metric tied to core value, showed a problem the volume number concealed.</p>\n<span id=\"c19-6-baseline-strong-standout\"></span>\n<h3>6. Baseline, Strong, Standout</h3>\n<p><strong>Prompt:</strong> &quot;Design the growth plan for a new marketplace that connects home cooks with local diners.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c19-baseline-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Baseline answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;It's a marketplace, so network effects will make it grow. I'd launch in as many cities as possible, spend on marketing to attract diners and cooks, and add features like reviews and a loyalty program.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>What is wrong:</em> &quot;network effects&quot; is a label; the plan spreads thin; no sense of what a successful match is, no sides, no risks.</p>\n<span id=\"c19-strong-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Strong answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;I'd map the two sides: cooks, who need a steady stream of diners and low hassle, and diners, who need reliable, safe, good food nearby. The cold start is the problem, so I'd launch in one neighborhood and seed cooks first, because without food diners leave. I'd measure liquidity: the share of diner requests that find a meal they want within a set time.</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd narrow the offering (a cuisine or a time of day) so that a few cooks can satisfy enough demand. Trust and safety matter in food: verification, food-safety rules and reviews. For growth, expand to the next neighborhood only when the first reaches a target match rate and repeat rate.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c19-standout-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Standout answer</h4>\n<p>Everything in the Strong answer, plus:</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd put numbers on density: if each cook can satisfy a given request 5% of the time, I need about 45 cooks in a market to get a 90% match rate, so 600 cooks should go into about 13 markets, not 30. I'd give cooks a reason to join before diners come: a tool for taking orders and payments, and a guaranteed minimum for the first months.</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd think about disintermediation: after a first meal, diner and cook can deal directly, so the platform must offer something continuing (payment protection, scheduling, insurance, reviews). I'd model the take rate against that risk.</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd check multi-homing: cooks can also sell through other channels, so loyalty comes from a steady flow of orders and low friction. Legal exposure is real (food-safety rules vary by place), so I'd involve legal early and pick the first market for its rules.</p>\n<p>&quot;Metrics: primary, successful meals per request; guardrails, food-safety incidents, disputes, time to match, and the share of orders from the top cooks. What would change my plan: a first neighborhood that reaches the match target slowly (then the offering is wrong) or a safety incident (pause and fix).&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c19-what-moves-an-answer-up-a-band\"></span>\n<h4>What moves an answer up a band</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>From Baseline to Strong</th>\n<th>From Strong to Standout</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Maps the sides and focuses on one market</td>\n<td>Computes the density needed and plans the number of markets</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Seeds the hard side and measures liquidity</td>\n<td>Addresses disintermediation, multi-homing and the take rate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Names trust and safety</td>\n<td>Names legal exposure, guardrails and what would change the plan</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c19-7-company-lens\"></span>\n<h3>7. Company lens</h3>\n<p>All claims describe what companies say publicly or what candidates commonly report, not insider knowledge. Interview formats change <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Apple [as of 2026-10].</strong> Apple runs an app store with published review guidelines and has changed its commission structure over time, including a reduced rate for small developers announced in 2020 <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Apple press release of 18 November 2020; App Review Guidelines on Apple's developer site]</code>. Third-party guides report that platform prompts at Apple concern review policy and the balance among developers, users and the platform <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: weigh speed, safety and developer trust together, discuss second-order effects on security and quality, and label regulatory facts as to be verified.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Google [as of 2026-10].</strong> Google develops a widely licensed mobile operating system and runs an app store with its own policies and a reduced fee tier on the first $1 million of developer revenue <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Google Android Developers Blog, 16 March 2021]</code>. Candidates commonly report platform prompts on openness and ecosystem health <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: discuss compatibility and fragmentation, partners and developers together, and the tradeoff between openness and security.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Microsoft [as of 2026-10].</strong> Microsoft's platforms include operating systems, cloud and productivity suites with partner programs and marketplaces <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Microsoft partner site: AI Cloud Partner Program; Microsoft Marketplace (AppSource and Azure Marketplace combined); posts to May 2026]</code>. Candidates commonly report enterprise-platform prompts on partner programs and revenue sharing <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: consider buyers, administrators and partners, tie marketplace economics to the suite's wider value, and mind compatibility with existing customers.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Amazon [as of 2026-10].</strong> Amazon operates a large third-party marketplace and a cloud platform <code>Public</code> <code>[V: European Commission decision on Amazon Marketplace; AWS]</code>. Candidates commonly report prompts on marketplace quality, seller trust and the tension of selling its own products alongside sellers' <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: address seller trust, rules on use of data and placement, and measure the health of both sides.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Meta [as of 2026-10].</strong> Meta restricted third-party access to its platform data in 2018 after a data-misuse controversy, an example of governance tightening in response to risk <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Facebook announcement of 4 April 2018 by Mike Schroepfer, as reported]</code>. Candidates commonly report prompts on developer access, privacy and platform policy <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: weigh developer value against user privacy and misuse, and describe how access is tiered, audited and revoked.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Netflix [as of 2026-10].</strong> Netflix is primarily a content service, not a two-sided platform, though it publishes open-source tools for other engineers <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Netflix Tech Blog posts on experimentation and resilience testing]</code>. Practical consequence: if a platform question concerns Netflix, check whether the platform lens fits before using it.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Stripe [as of 2026-10].</strong> Stripe's products are APIs for payments and related services, and it is widely described as emphasizing developer experience and documentation, and documents API versioning <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Stripe API versioning documentation and engineering blog]</code>. Candidates report developer-platform prompts <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: lead with the first integration, time to first call, error messages and stability.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c19-8-common-mistakes\"></span>\n<h3>8. Common mistakes</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Mistake</th>\n<th>How to detect it</th>\n<th>Fix</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>&quot;Network effects&quot; as a label</td>\n<td>No mechanism, locality or saturation</td>\n<td>Name the mechanism and its limits</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Designing for one side</td>\n<td>Only buyers or only developers</td>\n<td>Map every side's gives, gets and fears</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Launching broad</td>\n<td>Many markets, thin supply</td>\n<td>Compute the density threshold; concentrate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Counting sign-ups</td>\n<td>Growth measured by registrations</td>\n<td>Measure liquidity: successful transactions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>API as plumbing</td>\n<td>No funnel, no time-to-first-call</td>\n<td>Treat the first integration as the product</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Breaking changes without notice</td>\n<td>Developers surprised</td>\n<td>Version, deprecate with notice</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>One blanket review process</td>\n<td>All submissions treated alike</td>\n<td>Tier by risk; explain; allow appeals</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Take rate by revenue alone</td>\n<td>Rate cut or raised without a response model</td>\n<td>Model participation and the cost floor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Ignoring disintermediation</td>\n<td>Value is only the introduction</td>\n<td>Provide continuing value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>One volume metric</td>\n<td>GMV only</td>\n<td>Add quality, concentration and developer metrics</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c19-9-mental-models-applied\"></span>\n<h3>9. Mental models applied</h3>\n<p>Four models, each tied to a decision in this chapter. <a href=\"#gl-inversion\">inversion</a> is from C02.</p>\n<p><strong>Metcalfe's law, and its limits</strong> (<code>MM-metcalfe</code>). The idea that a network's value grows with the square of its users <code>[V: Metcalfe's law, as listed in software-engineering law references]</code> is a useful intuition for why large networks are attractive, and a poor guide to planning: value depends on who can connect to whom, networks are often local, and value per user can saturate. Use it to explain why scale matters and never to forecast.</p>\n<p><strong>The tragedy of the commons</strong> (<code>MM-commons</code>). A shared resource that anyone can use without cost tends to be degraded by overuse <code>[V: Hardin, Science 162(3859), 13 December 1968, pp. 1243 to 1248; the idea goes back to William Forster Lloyd, 1833]</code>. An open platform with no quality bar can be filled with low-quality or abusive participants who each benefit and together harm everyone. Governance (review, ratings, fees, enforcement) is the platform's answer.</p>\n<p><strong>Postel's law</strong> (<code>MM-postel</code>). Be conservative in what you send and liberal in what you accept <code>[V: Postel's law (robustness principle), as quoted in several sources]</code>. For API design: send clean, well-specified responses, and tolerate reasonable variation in what developers send, with clear errors when you cannot. It is a basis for developer-friendly interfaces and also a caution: leniency can lock in sloppy behavior that is hard to change later.</p>\n<p><strong>Inversion</strong> (<code>MM-inversion</code>, see <a href=\"#gl-inversion\">inversion</a>, C02). To design governance, ask how you would destroy the platform: make developers distrust it (arbitrary rules, sudden fee changes), flood it with low-quality supply, or let the largest participants take over. The list is the checklist of what to protect.</p>\n<span id=\"c19-engineering-design-and-data-science-perspectives\"></span>\n<h3>Engineering, design and data-science perspectives</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Engineering.</strong> Platform engineers own reliability, rate limits, versioning and security, and know what compatibility costs. Ask what breaking changes are in the pipeline and how they will be handled; ask for the error messages to be reviewed as product text.</li>\n<li><strong>Design and research.</strong> Research on both sides: sit with developers integrating for the first time; interview sellers and buyers. Design the seller or developer console as a product with its own users.</li>\n<li><strong>Data science.</strong> Two-sided markets complicate measurement: tests of a change on one side can affect the other (interference, C14.4), so market-level or cluster designs may be needed. Ask for liquidity measures and concentration measures, and for dashboards by side.</li>\n<li><strong>Policy, legal and trust and safety.</strong> Rules on fees, openness, competition and data are changing <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>; safety teams define what must be blocked. Involve them early in any change to rules, fees or access.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c19-checklist-before-you-recommend-a-platform-move\"></span>\n<h3>Checklist: before you recommend a platform move</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Have I mapped every side's gives, gets, alternatives and fears?</li>\n<li>Did I name the network-effect mechanism, its locality and its saturation point?</li>\n<li>For a new platform, did I compute the density needed and choose where to start?</li>\n<li>Did I treat the API as a product, with a funnel and a stability policy?</li>\n<li>Did I set governance as a three-way tradeoff (speed, safety, trust) and model the take rate against participation and cost?</li>\n<li>Did I scan for dependency, concentration, disintermediation, conflict with participants and regulatory attention?</li>\n<li>Is the primary metric tied to successful transactions, with guardrails across sides?</li>\n<li>Did I say what would change my recommendation?</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c19-10-case-studies\"></span>\n<h3>10. Case studies</h3>\n<span id=\"cs-c19-01\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C19-01 · The marketplace spread across eight cities · Illustrative · Beginner</h5>\n<p><em>Fictional composite. Every number is invented (invented).</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> A marketplace connecting households with cleaners has raised money and recruited 160 cleaners. To show reach, the team launches in eight cities at once, with 20 cleaners in each. After three months, the team sees many sign-ups by households and a low repeat rate.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> A fixed supply of 160 cleaners, a marketing budget, and a board expecting national coverage.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> For a household request, suppose each cleaner in the city can take the job with probability 4%. Of households that tried a booking in the first month, 44% did not get a match within a day (invented), and of those, 85% never tried again. Households that were matched rebooked at a healthy rate.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> As an observable problem: &quot;Households in each city often request a cleaning and find nobody available, and they do not come back.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> With 20 cleaners per city, the match chance is 1 − 0.96²⁰ = 56%, consistent with the 44% failure the team saw. A sign-up metric counted the failures as growth. If the same 160 cleaners were in 2 cities (80 each), the match chance would be 1 − 0.96⁸⁰ = 96%. Density per market, not the total, determines whether a household's first experience works, and the first experience determines repeat use. The cold-start playbook (FW-C19-03) says: narrow the market, seed the hard side, measure liquidity, expand when the gate is met.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Keep eight cities and add marketing</td>\n<td>Apparent reach</td>\n<td>Most requests fail; wasted spend</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Concentrate in two cities; pause the others with a waitlist</td>\n<td>Match rate and repeat use</td>\n<td>Disappoint six cities; board optics</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Recruit 500 more cleaners</td>\n<td>Density everywhere</td>\n<td>Cost and time; hiring quality risk</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> B gets the product working with the supply in hand and builds proof; C is slower and costly; A repeats the failure.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> Choose B. Put all 160 cleaners into the two densest cities. Primary metric: match rate within a day (target 90%); guardrails: time to match, cleaner earnings (so they stay), complaints; gate: expand to a third city when two cities have a 90% match rate and 30-day repeat above a threshold. Tell the board the plan and the gate.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> In this fictional scenario the match rate in the two cities rose to 95%, repeat bookings rose, and cleaners' earnings increased because they had more jobs (invented). The third and fourth cities launched four months later with 50 cleaners each, after recruiting.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> If the two cities had been poor for demand, a match rate of 95% with few requests would have shown that supply was not the constraint, and the team would have changed cities or marketing. If cleaners had left because of low earnings in thin markets, the earnings guardrail would have shown it. The lesson: density per market is the unit of progress, and a measure of liquidity is the honest dashboard.</p>\n<span id=\"cs-c19-02\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C19-02 · A mobile app store as a platform, reconstructed from public documents · Reconstructed · Advanced</h5>\n<p><em>Sources (types): Apple's press releases (the App Store's first-weekend announcement of 14 July 2008 and the App Store Small Business Program of 18 November 2020, with Apple's feature of the same day), primary sources for its statements and figures; Apple's published App Review Guidelines, read at the level of their structure and not quoted; Google's announcement of its reduced service fee (Android Developers Blog, 16 March 2021), a primary source for its statement; Apple's Form 10-Q for the quarter ended June 2026 (SEC), for the status of fee disputes; and reference summaries and press coverage for the launch day, the 70/30 split and the launch-day count of about 500 applications. The analysis is an inference from these public documents, labeled throughout, and is not based on internal data, deliberations or financials. Re-read in October 2026.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> The App Store launched on 10 July 2008 with roughly 500 applications, according to the count Steve Jobs gave on launch day as reported by the press, and a revenue split in which developers kept 70% of sales revenue and Apple kept 30% <code>[V: reference summaries quoting Apple's 2008 announcements]</code>. On 14 July 2008 Apple announced that users had downloaded more than 10 million applications in the first three days and that more than 800 were available <code>[V: Apple press release, 14 July 2008]</code>. As the catalog grew, discovery plausibly became a problem for developers; that is an inference from the scale (1.8 million apps by 2020), not a sourced statement <code>[inference]</code>. In November 2020, Apple announced a program, launching on 1 January 2021, under which developers who earned up to $1 million in proceeds (defined as post-commission earnings) in the previous calendar year would pay a reduced 15% commission, with requalification possible in later years, while the standard 30% remained for those above that level; in a feature published the same day Apple said that half a billion visitors a week engaged with 1.8 million apps <code>[V: Apple, 18 November 2020]</code>. Google announced on 16 March 2021 that from 1 July 2021 its Play service fee would be 15% on the first $1 million of revenue each developer earns each year, available to every developer using Play billing and returning to 30% beyond that amount <code>[V: Android Developers Blog, 16 March 2021; TechCrunch]</code>. Apple publishes App Review Guidelines arranged, in its words, into five sections (Safety, Performance, Business, Design and Legal), with notes on what to do before and after submitting <code>[V: Apple App Review Guidelines]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> A two-sided and developer platform: developers supply apps and need distribution, payments and trust; users need discovery, safety and ease; the platform sets the rules, funds itself through a commission and benefits from the ecosystem's effect on its devices. Public criticism and legal and regulatory scrutiny of fees and rules have been widely reported: in its filing for the quarter ended June 2026, Apple described a US court order barring it from charging commission on purchases made outside an app, an appeals court ruling that some commission may be charged on such link-out purchases with the case sent back to the district court, the Supreme Court's grant on 30 June 2026 of Apple's petition to review the legal standard for civil contempt, and European Commission investigations under the Digital Markets Act <code>[V: Apple Form 10-Q for the quarter ended 27 June 2026]</code>; the facts of specific disputes beyond that are outside the sources used here <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As reported: the dates, the revenue split, the first-weekend downloads, the later growth to 1.8 million apps, the 2020 small-business tier and its terms, the Google tier, and the discovery problem as the catalog grew. No internal data is used.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> <em>The author's reading.</em> The platform had to design a set of rules and prices that would attract developers, protect users and fund itself, while keeping a catalog discoverable as it grew. As an observable problem: &quot;Set the rules, the review and the commission so that developers keep building, users trust what they download, and the platform is paid for what it provides.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> <em>The author's analysis, as inference from public documents.</em></p>\n<p><em>Sides and loop (FW-C19-01).</em> Users buy devices partly for the apps; developers build for the devices because users are there and can pay; each side raises the value to the other, a cross-side effect that is local to the platform's own users and strengthened by the integration of device, software and store (an ecosystem). Developers can and do multi-home across platforms, so the platform has to keep the best terms of distribution, tools and reach. Inference: the early 70/30 split was a simple, attractive offer compared with the earlier mobile software market, which contemporary press described as controlled by carriers, which took 40% to 50% of revenue on apps distributed through their phones against Apple's 30% <code>[V: TechCrunch, 24 July 2008]</code>.</p>\n<p><em>Governance (FW-C19-05, FW-C19-08).</em> A review before listing protects users and the platform's reputation (safety, privacy, quality) and imposes delay and uncertainty on developers, which affects developer trust. The public guidelines and the scale of the catalog imply a tiered process in which automated checks handle routine submissions and humans handle harder cases; this is inference, since the internal process is not described in the sources used here.</p>\n<p><em>Take rate.</em> A flat commission is simple and predictable; its level is a lever with effects on developers' margins and prices. The 2020 small-business tier, by inference, targeted the many small developers (Apple said the program would benefit the vast majority of developers who sell digital goods and services <code>[V: Apple, 18 November 2020]</code>) in a way that cost relatively little revenue while improving the platform's standing with developers and in the regulatory conversation; Apple itself framed it as helping small businesses. Google's later tier is consistent with competitive and similar regulatory pressure, again an inference; it is also structured differently, since it applies to the first $1 million each developer earns each year with 30% beyond that, whereas Apple's tier depended on the previous year's proceeds <code>[V: Android Developers Blog; Apple, 18 November 2020]</code>.</p>\n<p><em>Ecosystem risk (FW-C19-06).</em> Dependency: developers who depend on the store for distribution are exposed to rule changes, and the platform depends on a few large developers for much of its revenue (concentration, a general feature of such stores). Disintermediation: where an app can sell digital goods through its own payment system, the platform's commission is at risk; rules on payment and on linking out are the subject of public disputes <code>[V: Apple Form 10-Q, 2026]</code>. Conflict with participants: the platform's own apps compete with third-party apps, and developers' concern about placement and data use is widely reported. Regulatory attention: the fee and openness rules draw scrutiny in several jurisdictions. These risks are public topics; the case does not assess the merits of any dispute.</p>\n<p><em>What the evidence does not show.</em> The platform's costs, margins or internal decision criteria; the effects of the 2020 tier on revenue; and how developers' behavior changed. The case supports a structural analysis only.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong> <em>The author's reconstruction of choices a platform owner could face; not a record of any company's deliberations.</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Keep a single flat commission and strict review</td>\n<td>Simplicity; consistency; revenue</td>\n<td>Developer dissatisfaction; regulatory exposure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Tier the commission (lower for small developers) and keep strict review</td>\n<td>Developer goodwill; modest revenue cost</td>\n<td>Complexity; large developers unaffected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Open alternative distribution and payment with safeguards</td>\n<td>Developer choice; regulatory fit</td>\n<td>Security and quality risk; revenue; consistency</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A is simple and draws criticism; B responds to small developers cheaply; C changes the platform's model and brings new risks. The choice among them depends on how much the platform's value rests on control and consistency, and on what regulators require.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> As documented, the platform launched with review and a 70/30 split, and in 2020 introduced a 15% tier for small developers.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> Reported: growth to 1.8 million apps and half a billion weekly visitors by late 2020 (Apple). Later developments are outside the sources used here.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> <em>Inference.</em> A looser review might have grown the catalog faster and increased the presence of harmful or low-quality apps, with costs to user trust. A lower initial commission might have attracted more developers and reduced the platform's funding for its own services. A reader should keep in mind that a platform's rules are hard to change once many businesses depend on them, which is why governance decisions are among the most consequential and why they deserve the three-way tradeoff analysis of this chapter.</p>\n<span id=\"c19-11-interview-questions-and-model-answers\"></span>\n<h3>11. Interview questions and model answers</h3>\n<p>Seven questions: two Beginner, two Intermediate, three Hard. Model answers are <code>Illustrative</code> (composite candidates), every figure is an assumption, and the arithmetic was computed. Each is written against the six behaviors in section 1. Where a real company's product is the setting, the answer reasons from public information and states no internal fact. The answers are fuller than a first pass in a real round.</p>\n<span id=\"iq-c19-01\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C19-01 · Beginner · strategy · ST</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> What is a platform? How is a two-sided market different from a developer platform?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;A platform is a big app that lots of people use.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Defines a platform by multiple participant groups and gives both types with examples.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Names the different core jobs and hard problems of each, notes hybrids, and says when the label does not apply.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> A platform is a product that others build on or transact through, which creates value for more than one group. The defining feature is that the value to one group depends on another group's participation.</p>\n<p>A <em>two-sided market</em> has two groups who need each other: riders and drivers, buyers and sellers. The platform's core job is matching and trust. The hard problems are the cold start (neither side comes until the other is there), keeping enough supply near the demand (liquidity), and disintermediation (participants bypassing the platform after a match). The primary metric is successful transactions.</p>\n<p>A <em>developer platform</em> provides a foundation that others build products on, through APIs and tools: an operating system, a cloud service, a payments API. The core job is to be reliable, well-documented and stable. The hard problems are developer experience (how fast a developer succeeds), stability (not breaking what people built), and governance. The primary metric is developers who ship to production, or what they build.</p>\n<p>Many platforms are both: an app store has developers (who build) and users (who buy), so it faces review-policy, fee and discovery problems on top of the two kinds of problem.</p>\n<p>When it does not apply: a product with many users but no interaction among them and no outside builders is just a popular product. Calling it a platform imports the wrong questions. My first step with a platform question is to name the sides and what each gives and gets.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Give an example of a hybrid, and say when the platform label does not apply. <strong>A.</strong> &quot;An app store is both: a two-sided market between users and developers, and a developer platform with tools, interfaces and rules. The label does not apply when no group's value depends on another group's participation. A company that offers an API to a few customers but whose product works the same whether or not anyone builds on it is a product with an interface, not a platform. The test I use is whether adding more participants of one kind makes the product more valuable to another kind.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What are the hard problems of each type? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;For a two-sided market: the cold start (each side waits for the other), liquidity (the chance a buyer finds a seller and a seller finds work), and trust between strangers. For a developer platform: interface stability so that builders can rely on it, a good developer experience (time to a first working call), and governance of what builders may do. The measures differ accordingly: liquidity and repeat transactions for the market, production-active developers and time to first call for the developer platform.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c19-02\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C19-02 · Beginner · strategy · ST</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> A freelance marketplace has 5,000 freelancers and 300 clients. Clients complain they cannot find the right person, and freelancers complain they get no work. What's going on, and what do you do?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Get more clients with marketing.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Diagnoses a supply-demand imbalance and a matching problem, and proposes narrowing.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Quantifies the ratio, uses liquidity, separates the two complaints, and proposes seeding demand and curating supply, with a metric.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The two complaints are one problem seen from each side. There are about 17 freelancers for each client (5,000 ÷ 300), so work is scarce for freelancers, and with so many to choose among and no structure, clients struggle to find the right person. The platform has too much unfocused supply and too little demand.</p>\n<p><em>Diagnose by segment.</em> Not all freelancers compete for the same work. Suppose most are in a few popular categories while demand is concentrated in a few others (invented). Look at the match rate and time to hire by category: some categories may be healthy, others empty on one side.</p>\n<p><em>What I'd do.</em> (1) <em>Narrow:</em> focus on the categories where demand exists and where the platform can reach density, and stop recruiting supply in saturated ones. (2) <em>Raise the quality of matching for clients:</em> better search, a short brief that routes to a small set of qualified candidates, so clients see a few strong options and not a thousand. (3) <em>Grow demand where supply is strong:</em> outbound to clients who need those skills. (4) <em>Curate supply:</em> verified skills and reviews, so clients can trust what they see and the best freelancers get the work, which keeps them.</p>\n<p><em>Metric.</em> Not sign-ups. Primary: the share of client briefs that result in a hire within a week (hire rate), with time to first qualified proposal, and freelancer earnings for the top and median freelancer as guardrails (if only a handful earn, supply will leave). I'd check that clients rehire.</p>\n<p><em>Risk.</em> Cutting supply feels like shrinking; the aim is balance. What would change my mind: if clients' real issue is trust and not selection, in which case verification and protection matter more than matching.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you quantify the imbalance and segment it? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;The ratio is about 17 freelancers per client. Then I would segment: if 60% of freelancers are designers and most client projects are for developers, the imbalance is concentrated in design. I would measure, by category, projects posted, proposals per project (for example 85 in design against 12 in development), the share of projects filled within seven days, and the share of freelancers who won any work in 30 days. This shows where supply is idle and where demand is unserved, and it turns two complaints into one table.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What would you do to seed demand and curate supply? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Pause freelancer onboarding in the oversupplied categories and focus on bringing in clients through outbound outreach and partnerships, since demand is the constraint. For clients, replace an open list with a matched shortlist of five and a quick way to invite them. The measure is the share of projects filled within seven days and the shortlist acceptance rate, with freelancer earnings per active freelancer as a guardrail. Reopen supply onboarding in a category only when proposals per project fall below a set level, for example 25.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c19-03\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C19-03 · Intermediate · strategy · ST · Stripe</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> A payments API team can ship a cleaner design that simplifies integration for new developers but breaks 3% of existing integrations. Do you ship it?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, simpler is better,&quot; or &quot;No, never break developers.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Weighs benefits for new developers against costs to existing ones, and considers versioning.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Quantifies both, proposes a non-breaking alternative (a new version alongside the old), a deprecation timeline and migration support, and treats trust as an asset.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The setting is general: I know such products only from public information, and nothing here describes any real plan. I'd not ship it as a breaking change on a flag day. I'd look for a way to get the benefit without the break.</p>\n<p><em>Numbers (invented).</em> 200,000 businesses have integrated; 3% is 6,000. Helping each migrate costs about $150 of support and engineering time: $900,000 once. The benefit: a simpler API raises the share of new developers who reach production from 56% to 62% (a better first mile, C19.3); with 20,000 new developers a year, that is 1,200 more integrations, each worth $2,000 a year in revenue (invented): $2.4 million a year. The risk: if 5% of the 6,000 affected businesses leave because of the disruption, 300 integrations are lost at $2,000: $600,000 a year. Net in year one: $2.4 million − $0.9 million − $0.6 million = +$0.9 million. That looks positive, but it ignores trust: a platform known for breaking integrations raises the cost of building on it for everyone, and that is hard to measure and costly to lose.</p>\n<p><em>Alternative.</em> Ship the cleaner design as a new version, and keep the old working. New developers get the simple path; existing integrations are untouched. Cost: maintaining two versions for a while. Then deprecate the old version on a published schedule (say 24 months), with notice, a migration guide and tooling, and focus support on the businesses most affected. This captures most of the $2.4 million without the $0.6 million risk and the trust cost.</p>\n<p><em>Decision.</em> Introduce the new version, keep the old, announce a long deprecation path, and measure adoption of the new version by new and existing developers, support tickets, and churn among those who migrate. What would change my mind: if the old design carries a security flaw, a shorter timeline with strong support is justified.</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Stripe is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of developer-platform prompts and <code>Public</code> that Stripe documents API versioning <code>[V: Stripe API versioning documentation; the prompt descriptions are candidate-reported and unverified]</code>. No Stripe data or plan is claimed.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What non-breaking alternative would you propose? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Ship the cleaner design as a new version alongside the old one, so existing integrations keep working. Offer migration tooling and a compatibility layer for the 6,000 affected integrations, announce a deprecation timeline of 18 to 24 months with reminders, and give the largest users direct help. New developers get the simple version by default. The old version stays supported until usage falls below a threshold, and I would measure the share migrated each quarter. This gets most of the benefit and protects the trust that developers place in the platform's stability.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you compute the case? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;With invented figures. Helping 6,000 integrations migrate at about $150 each costs $900,000 once. Suppose the simpler design lifts activation of new developers from 40% to 48% of 20,000 new developers a year, 1,600 more integrations, each worth $500 a year: $800,000 a year. Payback is just over a year before counting the risk. The risk of a flag-day break is that 2% of the affected developers leave and others lose trust, which could cost far more than the migration. The numbers favor a staged migration over a break.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c19-04\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C19-04 · Intermediate · strategy · ST · Microsoft</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> A software vendor accounts for 12% of your enterprise marketplace's revenue. It asks for a lower revenue share and a say in your roadmap. How do you respond?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Give it what it wants, it's too big to lose,&quot; or &quot;treat everyone the same.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Weighs the vendor's value and leverage against fairness to other vendors, and offers structured options.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes the cost, separates the economic request from the influence request, proposes volume tiers open to all and a partner council, and addresses concentration risk and the precedent.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The setting is general: I know such platforms only from public information, and nothing here describes any real plan. Two requests are bundled and need separating: price and influence.</p>\n<p><em>Price.</em> Suppose the marketplace's revenue is $50 million at a 20% share, so the vendor generates $6 million in revenue, on $30 million of sales. A cut to 12% would reduce revenue from that vendor to $3.6 million, a loss of $2.4 million a year. If other vendors learn of a special deal, they will ask for it, and trust in the rules erodes. If the vendor really generates 12% of revenue, it is also a concentration risk: its departure would hurt, which is a reason to reduce dependence as well as to keep it.</p>\n<p><em>Principled answer.</em> Offer a published volume tier that any vendor can qualify for, with a lower rate above a sales threshold, perhaps with a requirement that gives the platform something in return (co-selling, deeper integration, support commitments). The vendor gets a better rate because of its size, and every other vendor can see the rule and aim for it.</p>\n<p><em>Influence.</em> A seat on a partner advisory council, where several vendors give input to the roadmap, is reasonable; a private veto is not, because it would trade the platform's neutrality and its other vendors' trust for one account. I'd listen to the vendor's needs (the real product problems) and consider them on the same criteria as other requests.</p>\n<p><em>Concentration.</em> Work on the broader base: grow the number of mid-sized vendors and measure the share of revenue from the top 10 (target a decline over time).</p>\n<p><em>Decision.</em> Offer the volume tier and the council, hold on a bespoke deal, and give the vendor a dated plan for roadmap items that serve many vendors. What would change my mind: if the vendor is truly strategic (it brings customers that others do not), a bigger commitment from them in exchange for a deeper partnership, structured as a tier open to peers.</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Microsoft is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of enterprise partner and marketplace prompts at Microsoft <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Microsoft data or program is claimed.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you separate the price request from the influence request? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;They are different asks with different answers. On price: if the vendor generates $6 million at a 20% share on $30 million of sales, a 12% share would cut that to $3.6 million, a loss of $2.4 million, about 5% of the marketplace's $50 million revenue. I would not agree to a bespoke rate; I would offer volume tiers open to every vendor, for example 20% under $10 million, 16% from $10 to $30 million and 14% above, so that the vendor benefits and the precedent is a rule. On influence: a partner council with several vendors that advises on the roadmap, with published criteria, while decisions stay with the platform.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you handle concentration risk and the precedent? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By reducing dependence and making terms public. Twelve percent of revenue from one vendor is a risk in both directions: the vendor could leave, and the marketplace could be pressed into terms that hurt others. I would diversify by growing other vendors, agree a multi-year contract with notice periods, and have a contingency if the vendor moves its sales elsewhere. Any special term given to one vendor becomes a demand from all, so terms should be rules, not deals. I would also track vendor concentration as a standing metric.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c19-05\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C19-05 · Hard · strategy · ST · Apple</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Design the review policy for an app store: speed versus safety versus developer trust.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Review everything carefully,&quot; or &quot;make it fast.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the three goals and designs a tiered process.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Sorts submissions by risk, quantifies reviewer capacity, builds explanations and appeals, measures false rejections and harmful approvals, and sets governance for changes to the rules.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I know the topic only from public information; this describes a design for a store of the kind Apple and Google operate, not any real process. The three goals pull against each other (FW-C19-08). Strict review raises safety and costs speed and trust; light review raises speed and risk. The way through is to match scrutiny to risk.</p>\n<p><em>Tier by risk.</em> Suppose 100,000 submissions a week (invented). Low risk (about 70%): updates to established apps with no new permissions or content types. Medium (25%): new apps in ordinary categories and updates that add features. High (5%): new apps in sensitive areas (payments, children, health, financial products), apps requesting powerful permissions, and apps from new accounts with signals of abuse.</p>\n<p><em>Process.</em> Automated checks on everything (malware, policy violations, privacy declarations): minutes. Low risk: automated approval within an hour. Medium: automated checks plus a short human review, within 24 hours. High: deeper human review, two to three days, with specialists.</p>\n<p><em>Capacity check.</em> Human time: 25,000 medium submissions at 0.5 hours is 12,500 hours; 5,000 high at 3 hours is 15,000 hours; total 27,500 hours a week, about 786 reviewers at 35 productive hours each. That is the real cost, and why automation and good tiering matter: each point of submissions moved from medium to low saves hundreds of reviewer hours.</p>\n<p><em>Developer trust.</em> Published, specific rules with examples; consistent decisions (audit a sample for consistency); a reason with every rejection, tied to a rule; a fast appeals path with a different reviewer; a way to ask for guidance before submitting; predictable timing. Trust is lost mainly through opaque, inconsistent or slow decisions.</p>\n<p><em>Safety.</em> Post-release monitoring (reports, anomaly detection), fast takedown and a way to revoke developer access; stronger identity checks for developers who handle sensitive data.</p>\n<p><em>Measures.</em> Median and 95th-percentile time to decision by tier; harmful-approval rate (apps approved that later violate rules); false-rejection rate (rejections overturned on appeal), target under 1% of decisions; developer satisfaction; and user-reported harm. Guard against gaming: a faster median hides a long tail.</p>\n<p><em>Governance of the rules.</em> Changes announced with notice, a comment period for significant changes, and a review of rules against outcomes each quarter.</p>\n<p><em>Decision.</em> Tiered review with automation for the bulk, human depth where risk is high, explanations and appeals for trust, and the measures above. What would change my mind: a spike in harmful approvals (tighten the low tier) or a rise in overturned rejections (retrain reviewers or simplify rules). Regulatory requirements on app review differ by place and are changing <code>[V: Apple Form 10-Q, 2026; EU Digital Markets Act]</code>.</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Public</code> that Apple publishes review guidelines and <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that platform prompts at Apple include review policy <code>[V: Apple App Review Guidelines on Apple's developer site; the prompt descriptions are candidate-reported and unverified]</code>. No claim is made about any real review process, staffing or metrics.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you tier by risk and size reviewer capacity? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Three tiers. Low-risk updates (70% of 100,000 weekly submissions, invented) pass through automated checks with random sampling for audit; medium-risk submissions (25%) get a human review of about 8 minutes within 24 hours, which is 25,000 × 8 ÷ 60 = about 3,300 hours a week, or about 83 reviewers at 40 hours; high-risk categories such as payments, children, health and finance (5%) get a 45-minute deep review: 5,000 × 0.75 = 3,750 hours, about 94 reviewers. In total about 177 reviewers, so changing the tier shares changes the staffing. The numbers show where speed is bought and where safety is.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you measure false rejections and harmful approvals, and govern rule changes? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;False rejections by the appeal overturn rate and an audit of a sample of rejections; harmful approvals by post-release incidents per 1,000 apps and by user reports, with a review of each serious case. Add time to decision by tier and developer satisfaction. For governance: rule changes are published with notice (for example 60 days), explained with the reason, discussed in a developer council, and recorded in a change log. Developers can predict what will pass, and the owner can show that rules apply to everyone, including its own apps.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c19-06\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C19-06 · Hard · strategy · ST</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> A home-services marketplace suspects that customers and providers are booking repeat jobs off the platform. How do you detect and reduce this?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Ban contact information sharing&quot; or &quot;cut the fee.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Defines the problem, finds proxies to measure it, and offers value-based solutions.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Quantifies the leakage, shows that a fee cut alone may not pay, designs continuing value, considers fairness to providers, and sets a test and guardrails.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> Disintermediation is likely where the platform's value is the introduction and repeat jobs are easy to arrange directly. I'd start by measuring how big it is.</p>\n<p><em>Detection.</em> Direct measurement is impossible, so use proxies: pairs of customer and provider with a first job and then no further bookings though the customer keeps using the category (the customer's other bookings continue with someone else, or provider availability shows busy); attempts to share contact details in messages; repeat-booking rates lower than in comparable categories; providers whose reviews mention working &quot;outside the app.&quot; Estimate leakage: suppose (invented) 100,000 first jobs a month, 40% of pairs would rebook, and 30% of those rebookings leak. With a 15% take rate on a $60 job ($9), leakage costs 100,000 × 0.4 × 0.3 × $9 = $108,000 a month.</p>\n<p><em>Does cutting the fee on repeat jobs fix it?</em> Suppose repeat jobs are 40,000 a month; today 70% stay on-platform: 28,000 at $9 = $252,000. If a lower rate of 8% ($4.80) cut leakage from 30% to 10%, on-platform would be 36,000 at $4.80 = $172,800. Revenue falls. To keep revenue at $252,000 at $4.80 would need 52,500 repeat jobs on the platform, more than the 40,000 that exist. So a fee cut alone cannot pay; it must be paired with something else.</p>\n<p><em>What works: continuing value.</em> Things the customer and provider cannot easily replicate off-platform: payment protection and dispute resolution, insurance or guarantees on damage, scheduling and reminders, recurring bookings with automatic payment, a verified record of work and reviews that the provider builds and takes pride in, financing or faster payouts for providers. If a guarantee costs $1 per job and cuts leakage from 30% to 20%, on-platform repeat jobs become 32,000 at ($9 − $1) = $256,000, which slightly exceeds today's $252,000 and builds trust.</p>\n<p><em>Rules.</em> Contact-sharing bans have limited effect and annoy honest users; use them lightly for the first booking only, and focus on incentives.</p>\n<p><em>Test and guardrails.</em> Pilot the guarantee and recurring-booking tools in two markets with a holdout; measure repeat rate on-platform, provider earnings, customer satisfaction and the proxy for leakage. What would change my mind: leakage proving far smaller than the proxies suggest, in which case the effort belongs elsewhere.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you detect leakage? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;With proxies, since direct measurement is impossible. A customer and provider who had a first job and then no more bookings together while the customer keeps booking in the category with others; contact details in messages before booking; a lower repeat rate for pairs than in similar categories; and a short survey of providers and customers who stopped booking. I would estimate the leakage as the gap between the repeat rate we see and the rate in a comparable category where leakage is hard, and I would report it as a range.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Why might a fee cut alone not pay, and what continuing value would you add? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;A cut from 15% to 10% lowers revenue on every job, including the ones that do not leak, and leaks may continue if the platform adds nothing after the first job. Instead add value that persists: payment protection and a guarantee that only apply to jobs booked on the platform, insurance, scheduling and reminders, a record of work and reviews, and one-tap rebooking with a small loyalty reward after the third job. Test it in one city for eight weeks and measure repeat bookings per pair and provider satisfaction, with provider earnings as a guardrail so that fairness is protected.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c19-07\"></span>\n";
